-- title:
--   The hard kernel is continuous almost everywhere
-- statement:
--   For $r,R,a\in\mathbb R$, the sharply truncated Hilbert kernel $H_{r,R}(t)=\dfrac{\mathbf 1_{r<|t|}-\mathbf 1_{R<|t|}}t$ satisfies
--   $$
--   H_{r,R}\ \text{is continuous at}\ a+c\quad\text{for almost every }c\in\mathbb R .
--   $$
--
--   The kernel is discontinuous only at the finitely many points where $|t|\in\{0,r,R\}$. So it is continuous at almost every point of each line, as required for convergence of Riemann-type discretizations.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/OAI/Analysis/TriangularHilbert/SampledConvergence.lean#L58-L70

